          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "Current guidelines recommend that the first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure (hypertension) typically includes two main approaches: lifestyle changes and medication, with a particular emphasis on starting with drug treatment when necessary.\n\n1. **Lifestyle Measures**: For individuals with Stage 1 hypertension but without other risk factors, the initial approach may focus on lifestyle changes, with a follow-up assessment after three to six months before considering medication.\n\n2. **Medication**: High-risk individuals, including those with established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or a significant estimated risk of cardiovascular events, should begin drug treatment alongside lifestyle measures. \n\nThe first-line medications generally include:\n- Thiazide-type diuretics\n- Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\n- Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)\n- Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers\n\nImportantly, ACE inhibitors and ARBs should not be combined due to increased risk without added benefit (Document: \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\"). \n\nThese recommendations help ensure that treatment is both effective and individualized. Always consult with a clinician for personal advice tailored to specific health situations.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
